user_prompt:
Variables are listed below in random order. Use the exact id strings.
[
  {
    "id": "tub"
  },
  {
    "id": "smoke"
  },
  {
    "id": "lung"
  },
  {
    "id": "xray"
  },
  {
    "id": "dysp"
  },
  {
    "id": "asia"
  },
  {
    "id": "bronc"
  },
  {
    "id": "either"
  }
]

Task: propose a causal order prior. Earlier variables are potential causes or upstream conditions; later variables are downstream effects, measurements, symptoms, decisions, or aggregate outcomes.

Return one JSON object with this schema:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["exact id 1", "exact id 2", "..."],
  "confidence": 0.0,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "exact id", "after": "exact id", "confidence": 0.0}
  ],
  "rationale": "brief, no more than 80 words"
}

Constraints: ordered_nodes must contain every provided id exactly once; do not invent nodes; if evidence is weak, still provide the best semantic prior and lower the confidence.

raw_response:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["asia", "smoke", "tub", "lung", "bronc", "either", "xray", "dysp"],
  "confidence": 0.86,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "smoke", "after": "lung", "confidence": 0.95},
    {"before": "smoke", "after": "bronc", "confidence": 0.95},
    {"before": "tub", "after": "lung", "confidence": 0.9},
    {"before": "lung", "after": "either", "confidence": 0.93},
    {"before": "either", "after": "xray", "confidence": 0.9},
    {"before": "either", "after": "dysp", "confidence": 0.85}
  ],
  "rationale": "asia is a background risk factor, followed by exposures/diseases like smoking and tuberculosis. These can affect lung and bronchial conditions, which contribute to the latent diagnosis outcome either. xray and dysp are downstream measurement/symptom variables. This is a plausible semantic prior rather than a ground-truth causal ordering."
}
